  {
    "episode_id": 75023,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75023.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "You have to be idealistic enough to know what you're doing and ruthless enough to get it done. Correct. That's what leadership is. Has the US been ruthless enough with respect to China?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Leadership requires being idealistic enough to know what you're doing.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Leadership requires being ruthless enough to get things done.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Being both idealistic and ruthless defines what leadership is.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The United States has not been sufficiently ruthless toward China.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Ruthlessness is a legitimate and necessary trait in geopolitical strategy.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Idealism and ruthlessness are compatible and complementary traits in leaders.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The effectiveness of U.S. foreign policy toward China depends on its level of strategic ruthlessness.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "It is meaningful and assessable to judge nations by their degree of 'ruthlessness' in international relations.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that the current state of U.S.-China relations warrants a more aggressive approach.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 75023,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75023.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "So let me back up having authored that quote. It was in response to how do you define leadership. And I think if you look at great leaders, they're idealistic enough to know why they're doing what they're doing. And ruthless or tough enough then to get it done. and leadership leaders, effective leaders, people that have taken of note are those who do both. A lot of times journalists, commentators, historians talk about one side as if that's the defining characters, but you can have all the ideas you want, but if you don't have the capability. to see one through. And if all you do is see one through but you don't know why you're doing what you're doing or where it's leading to. So there's a compass to this as well as a perseverance to this. So I do think the United States not consistently One, on President Biden, particularly, I'm serving now, has a real vision. And we're in the middle, you and I are doing this meeting two weeks out from the state visit, where I think we're transforming our relationship in the region from a hubs and spokes to this lattice-like work system. And if you look at the last two years of whether it's encouraging Japan to step up on the security front, encouraging the trilateral relationship, a re-energized quad At the back end of our state visit by Japan, we'll have a first ever try that with the Philippines. The Aukis, you look at Vietnam. This lattice work architecture is a vision. It's a set of ideas. And it's been with a perseverance and determination led by the president in direct response to, I think, China's mistakes and in their own neighborhood, in their own backyard, which is actually a fundamental principle of theirs, which is China, Russia, the United States, as superpowers should have their own spheres of influence. What I think gets at them is that in their own backyard, their sphere of influence is limited by their mistakes and are taking advantage of it. So, is it ruthless enough? The President, I don't know if I would use Ruthless as the author of that word, but he has been persistent in pursuing a singular goal with a single-mindedness. And if you look at where we were five years ago and how people like you were talking about the United States, you'll get us today. I'd rather have our hand the way we're playing it than China's hand the way they're playing it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
