  {
    "episode_id": 75023,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75023.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Well, so let me step back in. I can't believe I'm taking this approach, but for the rare moment to be the more thoughtful here. One is not to be so aggressive that all you have is aggression. I think the United States, I wouldn't say, has a luxury, but should be strategic enough to decide, since we're in baseball season, which pitches to swing at it, which ones you don't swing at. And just to say your road's not taken, Well, you don't have to take every road. If you took every road, you wouldn't go anywhere fast. So I think it's taking the right roads, or swinging at the right pitches, the ones that count, the ones that have strategic value. I think the biggest change, I would say, or if I could say two notes. China has a basic premise. One, that they are the rising power, or declining power, and either you get in line, or you're going to get the Philippine treatment. That's basically you could distill it all down here in the neighborhood. Our message is we are a permanent Pacific power and presence and you can bet long on us. And I think when you look back at the two of those, we have made more of a down payment and are in stronger position on our basic premise than they are. And the other piece of this on the flip side We did stay too long with the idea that China was a strategic competitor when they had changed by 2013 using what Xi had said, going back to your first quote, where they approached the United States as a strategic adversary. And not really, you can maybe say Trump, but not until President Biden put in place the things that he has done, both on the diplomatic side, the defense side, the deterrent side, the political side. Did we have an effective strategy to deal with China's decision to approach the United States as an adversary rather than a competitor?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 75023,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75023.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "So Japan and Korea, I think, from my mind, the most impressive accomplishment in American diplomacy over this administration. How did it happen? What lessons should future leaders learn from this and maybe compare it to Clinton's diplomacy and getting other people who really hate each other to get along?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker considers this diplomatic achievement to be the most impressive accomplishment of American diplomacy during this administration.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Japan and Korea have achieved a significant diplomatic breakthrough under this administration.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker wants to understand how the diplomatic progress between Japan and Korea occurred.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes future leaders should derive lessons from this diplomatic success.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker suggests comparing this diplomacy to Bill Clinton's diplomatic efforts.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Diplomatic achievements involving historically adversarial nations like Japan and Korea are rare and noteworthy.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "American leadership played a central role in facilitating the improved relations between Japan and Korea.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Achieving cooperation among nations with deep-seated animosity is a valid measure of diplomatic success.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "There are transferable strategic insights from successful diplomacy that can inform future foreign policy decisions.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views historical enmity between Japan and Korea as a meaningful obstacle that has been overcome.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Bill Clinton's foreign policy was effective at reconciling hostile parties, making him a relevant benchmark.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The audience recognizes the significance of U.S.-led diplomacy in East Asia.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 75023,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75023.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Well, a lot of people, so I want to not that you were saying this, to dispel something. Some people think diplomacy is right over here, grey flannel salute, pipes, big thoughts. And some people think there's this dirty thing called politics over here. The fact is diplomacy is politics by just another name. And one of the lessons to take away from this is that President Biden showed great appreciation in building personal relations with both President UN and Prime Minister, and gave them then the confidence to go past doing the bare minimum. And that's an outgrowth of that. Second is actually, this is, if you look at past efforts of every other president who's tried this in administration, we put ourselves so far in the middle that Japan and Korea didn't talk to each other. And to the credit of the State Department, the president, the national security office, the ambassadors, Phil Goldberg, myself, we held going into Camp David a year out, almost 50 different trilateral gatherings at multiple different levels. that people got started to get, you know, at those trilateral gatherings, you have a lot of bilaterals. And Japan and Korea, you know, deputy foreign ministers, national security advisers, defense, they had meetings. And the constant contact created familiarity. And Camp David was the culmination of a year's worth of trilateral meetings at multiple levels where you're then having breakfast or lunch or coffee with your counterpart. And we didn't have to be in everything. Our role was more, at one level, not all, facilitate, but not let the two parties talk to each other through us. And that showed, I think, a maturity in how we approach our role, rather than kind of jumping into the deep end all the time. Sometimes we did, but a lot of times we did. The other piece of it is I think both you I think appreciating you know if you could and this is somewhat separate but relevant is for Mr. Kushida had a background with the 2015 agreement with Korea. President Yun's father comes to Japan in the 60s to study. Well, as you're here from Korea in the 60s, you're like a pioneer. And so both of them had a personal relationship beyond just professional. And they both realized, given what was going on with both China and North Korea, they had to do more than the old, what the two countries were doing. We wanted to do more. And I think so, when you put all those different threads together, you got something going.",
