    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "you know, you're, you're, I'm a big believer in it. And I'll give you one anecdote story. So when President Clinton, early in his tenure of his first term, Richard Nixon, I think, makes his first trip back to the White House ever. Clinton meets with him. One of the devices that Nixon gives, Clinton, he says, don't ever underestimate the power of the personal relationship. The State Department can't pick up the phone as I think he used the anecdote. Get Hosni Mubarak on the phone and say, I need this, this, and this is the President of the United States. And he's going to, if you have that relationship, he's going to owe you one because you've done X, Y, and Z for him. And can it do everything? Can it smooth over everything? No. Is it at crucial moments, a valuable to have that personal relationship and that personal experience and that trust without a doubt, a hundred percent? And I've seen it many, many times, whether it was on President Clinton's side in many crucial moments of whether it's the mid-east when we navigated India and Pakistan. I've seen it by President Obama on different pieces of his tenure. So, it's not everything, but as my grandfather would say, it's not Bupkis either.",
