    "turn_text": "But you can't have a 250,000 people with personality. For a color to stand out, you need a little gray somewhere. So here's, look, My big thing is y'all who cover it think diplomats are in one place and political people are in another. And I would say to you, if you go back and look at history, and look at people that were very successful on the international arena. They have a pretty apt appreciation of politics. Jim Banker comes to mind, but you could also go back to John Burns under both Roosevelt and Truman, who was a pretty shrewd political player when you'd go underwritten about but underappreciated how influential it was with those two presidents. So not everybody can be colorful and not, nor do you want everybody to be colorful. On the other hand, you don't want to create, well, you don't want to create as a permissive environment where it's so risk free. All you're doing is writing memos. You know, you don't want 100 people trying to go create things. On the other hand, you don't want to distill it down to the basis where you totally penalize anybody to either take a risk or try to make something. The goal here It's not to get another meeting to write another memo to write another meeting to write another memo. The goal here is, like in other jobs I've had with I said here when I first got here, we're going to get it on a plane at some point. You're going to turn around and you want to make sure that when you were here you put your thumb on the scales and you flipped it. It's not just to write a memo about a meeting.",
