    "turn_idx": 94,
    "turn_text": "this or that? So we get tarp is passed under Bush. We have this big fight in the Obama years. And it fights, you know, how to interpret it, how to implement it, et cetera. We in the early days, very early days of the President's administration passed what the stimulus bill, the Recovery Act, whatever you want to call it. That's another $800 billion. Nobody have ever done dollars like these two. $800 billion here, $750 billion is big money in six months. Yeah. There was a big discussion in the White House then about the three initiatives by the President. One is health care, one is financial banking reform, and the other one is climate. And I was, when these are what do you make decisions about? And, you know, at that point, just as the stimulus bill is passing, you're beginning to get the first Tea Party. I argued strongly, and we compared it to the President's credit, he asked for a meeting, we had a big discussion. I argued for doing financial reform first, because I thought that the political system, the society needed to see bankers held accountable. And I thought, as I said then, and I repeat, my quote was, we need some Old Testament justice. That's what the country needs. You know, put the bankers in the middle of public square and just literally beat the crap out of them. and reform the system that needed reform. Appropriately, people said that if we didn't do healthcare now, the clock would run out on healthcare, and if you look at the Clinton era, that's true. My argument against doing that was first, was unlike in financial reform, bankers were gonna be on the other side of the table. Two past healthcare, the lesson out of Clinton's tenure, was the health insurance industry, the doctors, the lawyers, doctors, hospitals, and pharmaceutical industry had it brought to your side of the table. So you didn't have a natural enemy except for the system. And the president made the calculation between time, politics, policy, goals, objectives. He went health care first and the kind of early stages of the tea party explodes and you feel it in the 2010 midterms. But you also feel it beyond. And you could argue, which is my view, and that doesn't mean I'm right. I'm just giving you my view. I think I'm right. that had we done something that dealt with the unaccountable financial industry and holding them accountable for having done what they did to people's homes and their value in their retirement, their economic security. Our American politics would not be filled with the rage resentment that it's filled with today. Now, I'm saying that on the eve of the anniversary of healthcare reform, and it's better that people have healthcare But like one of the things that is not always appreciated, everything is weighing equities. Lots of people have health care. Lots of children have health care as a son of a pediatrician. Children have health care. It's been better for their health. I'm not sure it's been that great for the political health of the system. And at the time, this little thing called the Tea Party has mysticized and grown to a politics filled with rage and anger and resentment. do I think I think what I was proposing would have dealt with that at an early stage. Could I, did I predict then that it would come to where we are today? No. So that was a road not taken. I think the second road not taken and I joined President Clinton in this and is, you know, I think the the Palestinian people are better than the Palestinian leadership. The Palestinian leadership has constantly used the Palestinian people, their aspirations, and their desires for their own power rather than for their own aspirations. And what was missed at Camp David when Ahu Barak And yes, our offer was there. What was missed when right on the heels of Oslo, the bombing of buses in Tel Aviv, while you had just made a peace agreement, destroyed any element. I mean, it's hard to think that in Israel in the early 90s, there was support over 60% for a two-state solution. Today, you can get two cents for the two-state solution. When you think about either putting down the gun and making peace work, or in the final days of the President's administration at Camp David to try to come to the agreement that RFOT said no to, I think those are roads not taken, but not by us, that we not only gave a road, we gave a GPS system, Google, we filled up the tank, we were the Uber driver, we did everything you could. But no, I'm serious. I think when you look at, you're doing this interview five months. Look at 2006, Hamas gets elected. It wasn't a single Israeli inside Gaza. They could have built not a tunnel. They could have built a school. They chose tunnels. They could have built hospitals. They chose tunnels. At some point, it's a road not taken.",
